@flexiarg thesis/join-two-theses-as-question-and-answer
@title Join Two Theses as Question and Answer
@status accepted 2026-11-11: proposed by 象 for 2 turns (Joe 2026-11-11: recurrence suffices); attested once used in a mission or a War Machine cascade
@provenance 象 proposals, ~/.emacs-graph/session-turn-analysis/<record>[.json].candidates.json; records: turn-ElMAv4 turn-yXlhrr; first proposed for: Two documents carry different theses bearing on the same goal: R1 (knowledge economy accessible to everyone) and Joe's zone-site cascade (can a community leave a self-steering account of itself).
@why [structure/two-projections-of-one-quantity]

! conclusion: Compose them explicitly: let one thesis supply the question and the other the answer-direction, then ask what current work does about the joined form.

  + IF:
    Two theses from different workstreams each look partial, and one reads as a question while the other reads as a desired outcome.

  + HOWEVER:
    Treating them as rivals forces a choice; merging them wholesale loses the distinction between the question and the answer.

  + THEN:
    Compose them explicitly: let one thesis supply the question and the other the answer-direction, then ask what current work does about the joined form.

  + BECAUSE:
    Two projections holds that two formulations can be views of one underlying thing; this specialises it to theses: the join reveals question and answer as projections of the same research aim.
